Nested virtualization - hands-on
================================

Six scripts. Nothing here is locked down; this is your own account, so read
or change anything you like.

  0-what-can-this-host-do.sh      what this machine is, and whether it is ready
  1-run-nested-demo.sh            the automatic demo   (~2 minutes)
  2-step-through.sh               the step-through demo (interactive)
  3-run-nested-demo-all-cores.sh  demo 1, but every guest gets every core
  4-find-the-core-ceiling.sh      demo 1 at 1, 2, 3 ... cores, to find where
                                  it stops working (long; hours on a big host)
  5-many-nested-at-once.sh        several stacks side by side, each a guest
                                  booting a guest inside itself

Demo 1 answers "does it work" and gets out of the way: it boots a guest,
that guest boots a guest inside itself, and the inner one prints
NESTED_DEMO_L2_OK.

Demo 2 is the one to sit with. It boots Layer 1 and hands you the console.
At each layer you can look around and then descend:

    uname -a           where am I
    cat go-deeper.sh   read what the next step does
    sh ./go-deeper.sh  descend one layer
    poweroff           climb back up one layer

Every run takes a private copy of the disk, so you can break things freely.

Demo 3 is demo 1 run wide. Demos 1 and 2 give each guest a single vCPU,
because that is the configuration they reliably complete in. Demo 3 gives
both the L1 guest and the L2 guest inside it every core this host has, which
means a second processor has to be brought up through two layers of
hypervisor. That is the part still being stabilised.

Demo 3 does not always finish, and how it fails is the interesting part. So
far: 64 vCPUs per guest completed on AMD; 2 completed on both AMD and Intel;
8 and 16 on Intel aborted the inner guest with a VM-entry failure; and 4 on
one Intel host panicked the host itself. Run it on a machine you can afford
to lose, and when it fails, keep the console log it names at the end.

Script 4 is the one to leave running. It sweeps the width upward and records
what happened at each step, and it writes each width to its log BEFORE the run
rather than after - so if a wide guest takes the machine down, the last line of
the log names the width that did it. Start it again after the reboot and it
picks up where it left off, marks that width HOST-PANIC, and stops rather than
proving the same thing twice.

Scripts 1 to 4 all go DOWN — one guest, inside one guest. Script 5 goes
ACROSS: it starts several of those stacks at the same time. That is a
different question, and it fails differently. Depth exercises the address
translation and the exit reflection; width exercises everything shared
between vCPU threads on the host, which is where contention shows up.

    sh 5-many-nested-at-once.sh           four stacks
    sh 5-many-nested-at-once.sh -n 8      eight
    sh 5-many-nested-at-once.sh -n auto   as many as this host has room for

It works out what the machine can hold — memory, disk for each private image
copy, cores — and refuses to start more than that. A host driven into swap
measures the swap device, not the hypervisor.

Any width you like, on either demo:

    sh 1-run-nested-demo.sh -c 4       four vCPUs per guest
    sh 1-run-nested-demo.sh -c all     every core
    sh 2-step-through.sh   -c 2        two vCPUs per layer

The real work happens in the root-owned launchers, which you can read:

    less /usr/local/libexec/cloudbsd-demo/run-auto-demo
    less /usr/local/libexec/cloudbsd-demo/run-stepthrough
    less /usr/local/libexec/cloudbsd-demo/nested-demo.sh

Hosts differ. Some are AMD (SVM), some Intel (VMX), some are themselves
virtual machines whose own host does not hand them the hardware, and not
every machine has the nested build installed. Script 0 tells you which case
you are in rather than letting a demo fail obscurely.
